# JULESHOTC TEST MACROS README -*- mode: org; buffer-read-only: t -*-
#+TITLE: JHTEST
#+AUTHOR: juleshotc <julesdelacruz49@gmail.com>
#+OPTIONS: toc:t num:nil

* Requirements

This utility was tested using GCC, and using the =-std=gnu23= flag.
Listed below are all the requirements necessary to build a project
that makes use of JHTEST.

- C23 Standard :: Use a C compiler that is compliant with the C23
  standard.

- GNU Extensions :: Use a C compiler that implements GNU extensions,
  specifically the =__attribute__((constructor))= extension.

- =JHTEST_IMPLEMENTATION= :: Call =#define JHTEST_IMPLEMENTATION= in
  one source file.

* Summary

This is a single header library that provides testing and assertion
macros.  You are provided four macros to use.

- =JHTEST= :: A macro to decorate functions with which you want
  running before =main()= for testing.

- =jhtest()= :: A function macro that expands to an =<assert.h>=
  assert outside of a =JHTEST= function and like a unit testing call
  inside of a =JHTEST= function.

- =JHTEST_IMPLEMENTATION= :: An stb-style macro that must be defined
  in one file.

- =NDEBUG= :: A conventional macro (borrowed from =<assert.h>=) that,
  when defined, disables any and all tests and asserts.  Define this
  during a release build.

* Internals

JHTEST makes use of constructors (a GNU extension), which run before
=main()= is called.  Priority levels 500, 501, and 502 are reserved
for JHTEST internal use.

| Priority | Behavior                                                                                                               |
|----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|      500 | Initialize behavior, then toggle a flag such that all calls to =jhtest()= are dispatched to the unit testing handler. |
|      501 | Run all =JHTEST= functions.                                                                                            |
|      502 | Provide a report, then un-toggle a flag such that all calls to =jhtest()= are dispatched to the assertion handler.    |

* Example Usage

#+BEGIN_SRC c
int foo(int a, int b)
{
  jhtest(a > 0);
  jhtest(b < 0);
  /* ... */
}
#+END_SRC

If the above jhtest calls fail, the program will log that and abort.

#+BEGIN_SRC c
JHTEST static void
test_foo(void)
{
  jhtest(foo( 11,  21) ==  31);
  jhtest(foo(-12,  22) == -32);
  jhtest(foo( 13, -23) == -33);
  jhtest(foo(-14, -24) == -34);
}
#+END_SRC

If any of the above jhtest calls fail, the program will not terminate.
It will simply log the error and continue, at least until it hits the
priority 502 constructor.  Only then will the program terminate.

* Recommendations

- =JHTEST= static :: Decorate all =JHTEST= functions static.  static
  was not added to the definition of =JHTEST= for clarity.  It is
  clearer to the programmer who has no idea what =JHTEST= is what is
  going on when the function is explicitly decorated static.

- Function naming :: On logging, JHTEST divides tests by function name
  for clarity.  Ideally name all your =JHTEST= functions something
  different to fully take advantage.  Otherwise, if two =JHTEST=
  functions are called sequentially and they both have names, they
  will have no header to act as a division between them.

